<p>Two Thousand Twenty-Three was the last year the league answered to the
<em>Whiny Little Bitch League</em>, and it went out exactly as it lived: with the
commissioner stealing a playoff spot he had no earthly right to, a doctor putting
up the best single game of the season on his way to dead-last, and the beginning
of a dynasty nobody wanted. It was also the last full season before the
weekly-high hundred existed, which means every dollar that year came the
old-fashioned way — by finishing high or by getting lucky. Mostly, that year, by
getting lucky.</p>

<h3><span class="n">§</span> The Fraud, and He Runs the League</h3>

<p>Let's not bury it. <strong>Cory</strong> — commissioner, and the proud owner
of a team named <em>Herbert the Pervert</em> — went <span class="stat">9–6</span>,
grabbed third place and <span class="stat">$400</span>, and did it with the
<em>single worst-performing roster of any team that saw a dollar.</em> Score his
lineup against the whole league every week — the all-play, the only honest math —
and Cory was a <span class="stat">.422</span> team, a <span class="stat">57–78</span>
outfit, sixth-best at actually scoring points. He converted that into the
fourth-best record in the league. That is not a season, that's a heist. He
outscored nobody and out-<em>lucked</em> everybody, and then, being the
commissioner, he certified the results himself. The fix was in and the fixer was
the ref. Fitting, then, that the man would spend the next year building a website
to make sure everyone's fraud got written down forever — starting, in fairness,
with his own.</p>

<h3><span class="n">§</span> The Robbed and the Cursed</h3>

<p>While Cory was laundering a .422 into playoff money, <strong>Justin</strong>
was getting mugged in broad daylight. <em>Rolling with Mahomies</em> was a
genuine <span class="stat">71–64</span> all-play team — a <span class="stat">.526</span>
squad that had every business making the bracket — and the schedule handed him
<span class="stat">6–9</span> and <span class="stat">$0</span> for his trouble.
Biggest gap between deserved and got in the whole league, and not a cent to show
for it. And then there was <strong>Jeremy</strong>, whose 2023 is almost too
cruel to write down: <em>Team Full of Jack Goff</em> went <span class="stat">5–10</span>,
lost <span class="stat">seven straight</span> at one point — the longest skid of
the year — and yet employed the man who posted the <em>best single-player game of
the entire season</em>, C.J. Stroud's <span class="stat">51.80</span> in Week 9.
The doctor had the biggest weapon in the league and finished tied for last. Hold
that thought. It matters in about twelve months.</p>

<h3><span class="n">§</span> El Travesty, Indeed</h3>

<p><strong>David</strong> named his team <em>El Travesty</em> and then spent the
season being exactly that. He posted the <em>highest score of the year</em> —
<span class="stat">171.50</span> in Week 4 — and used a chunk of it to bury
<strong>Sam</strong>'s <em>Lamb to the Slaughter</em> by <span class="stat">98.40</span>,
the fattest beatdown of the season, a score so lopsided the team name filed it
under truth in advertising. And yet, being the German, he found a way to suffer:
he dropped a <span class="stat">140.76</span> — the highest score anyone put up in
a losing effort all year — straight into a loss to Michael, and he missed a
weekly high by <span class="stat">0.52</span>. Oh, and he benched Justin Fields
for <span class="stat">40.98</span> in a game where his starters mustered
<span class="stat">69.12</span>. He led the league in points and in
self-inflicted wounds. Two things can be true.</p>

<h3><span class="n">§</span> The Hump, and the Rest of the Wretched</h3>

<p><strong>Dylan</strong> — <em>Schmelley</em>, the man who names the team after
himself and then loses with it — actually got somewhere in 2023: all the way to
the championship game, where he came second and pocketed <span class="stat">$550</span>.
It remains the closest he has ever come to getting over the hump, which is to say
he got within one game and then did what he does. <strong>Marian</strong> branded
himself <em>THE unanimous G.O.A.T.</em> and went <span class="stat">8–7</span> on
the second-luckiest ledger in the league — a man crowning himself while quietly
being carried by the schedule. <strong>Bates</strong>' <em>Red Rocket Rentals</em>
opened the year with a <span class="stat">52.20</span>, the lowest score of the
whole season, and never really recovered, finishing 5–10. <strong>Richard</strong>
went 6–9 and, mercifully, would have to wait two more years for his true
suffering. And <strong>Sam</strong> took that 98-point beating and still didn't
finish last, which around here counts as a win.</p>

<h3><span class="n">§</span> The Champion</h3>

<div class="respect">
  <p class="crown">The One Paragraph of Respect</p>
  <p><strong>Michael</strong> — <em>Dixie Wrecked</em> — won 2023 the honest way,
  and it was the first brick of a dynasty. He finished <span class="stat">10–5</span>
  and, unlike the man who finished a game behind him, he earned every inch: a
  <span class="stat">95–40</span> all-play record, the best in the league by the
  honest measure, and the highest points-for total of the year at
  <span class="stat">1,852.22</span>. No schedule charity, no laundered luck —
  just the best team, week in and week out, closing it out for the ring and
  <span class="stat">$1,050</span>. It was the first of what would become two
  titles in three years. The dynasty started here, and it started clean.</p>
</div>

<p>So the Whiny Little Bitch League signed off in character: the champion earned
it, the doctor wasted a monster and bottomed out, David led the league in points
and heartbreak, and the commissioner turned the worst good-team-record in the
league into playoff cash and then went home to build the machine that would rat
him out for it. The full accounting is below, in the only column this league has
ever actually cared about.</p>
